WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:08.699
This is the Korea Daily Brief from the CSIS Korea Chair, read by an AI voice. It's Monday, October 26.

2
00:00:08.699 --> 00:00:20.125
Three things today: North Korean howitzers aimed south for a second straight day, Kim Jong Un's surprise trip to China, and a new credit milestone for the Korea-U.S. investment vehicle.

3
00:00:20.125 --> 00:00:31.241
North Korea's frontline guns are still pointing toward South Korea this morning, and Seoul's defense minister says he's watching closely but won't say why Pyongyang put them there.

4
00:00:31.241 --> 00:00:41.740
The National Assembly defense committee is in session today for its comprehensive audit, with those howitzers and wartime operational control transfer both on the agenda.

5
00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:45.524
First, the howitzers on the western border.

6
00:00:45.524 --> 00:00:57.541
For the second consecutive day, cameras have captured North Korean 122-millimeter self-propelled howitzers at the Kaepung County coastal area of Hwanghae Province, barrels directed toward South Korea.

7
00:00:57.541 --> 00:01:09.859
Defense Minister Kang Shin-chul appeared before the National Assembly defense committee this morning and said the deployment carried, in his words, "various intentions," and that he was "watching closely."

8
00:01:09.859 --> 00:01:22.718
He added that no unusual movements had been observed beyond the positioning itself. The propaganda slogans visible on the equipment give the deployment a performative quality, but the military significance is real.

9
00:01:22.718 --> 00:01:35.817
This comes four days after Kim Yo Jong warned on October 22 that new response systems along the full border line would be placed on mission-standby. The howitzers fit that framing. North Korea sent a letter to the U.N.

10
00:01:35.817 --> 00:01:48.195
Security Council president accusing South Korea of staging the demilitarized zone land mine explosion that injured South Korean troops. Seoul had already sent its own letter blaming Pyongyang for the blast.

11
00:01:48.195 --> 00:01:57.509
Both governments are now trading accusations before the U.N. body. North Korea's state media released a statement on the letter. And separately, Daily N.K.

12
00:01:57.509 --> 00:02:07.424
reports that Pyongyang is using the blast, which it claims was fabricated, to justify intensified military drills for high school students in North Pyongan Province.

13
00:02:07.424 --> 00:02:19.621
At today's National Assembly audit, Kang also addressed wartime operational control transfer, saying Seoul and Washington are closely coordinating on setting a target year, without specifying a timeline.

14
00:02:19.621 --> 00:02:32.360
The bottom line for you: howitzers at the western border, a U.N. letter calling the mine blast a fabrication, and intensified student military drills. Pyongyang is running multiple pressure tracks simultaneously.

15
00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:35.865
Second, Kim Jong Un's trip to Dandong.

16
00:02:35.865 --> 00:02:48.542
The Wall Street Journal reports that Kim Jong Un made a surprise day trip to Dandong, the Chinese border city, crossing the new Yalu River bridge in what appears to be its first use by the North Korean leader.

17
00:02:48.542 --> 00:02:59.946
Kim met with Chinese Foreign Minister Wang Yi during the visit, confirmed by reporting cited by North Korea's state news agency. North Korean Foreign Minister Choe Son Hui accompanied Kim.

18
00:02:59.946 --> 00:03:09.833
During the visit, Kim said North Korea is, quote, "inspired" by China's success, framing the trip around the 76th anniversary of China's entry into the Korean War.

19
00:03:09.833 --> 00:03:26.574
Beijing's Foreign Ministry spokesman Guo Jiakun said at today's regular briefing that maintaining and developing China-North Korea relations was Beijing's, quote, "firm and unwavering strategic policy," and that stable bilateral ties benefited both peoples and regional peace.

20
00:03:26.574 --> 00:03:35.916
Guo declined to say whether the visit was initiated by China or proposed by North Korea. There is economic symbolism embedded in the crossing itself. N.K.

21
00:03:35.916 --> 00:03:47.926
News reports that the Dandong factory Kim toured, Huanghai Automobile, previously produced vehicles rebranded for the North Korean market and has signaled interest in expanding North Korea business.

22
00:03:47.926 --> 00:04:00.785
The Financial Times connects the bridge crossing to warming bilateral trade and to a parallel KPA commanders conference, reading the trip as a dual signal of external alignment and domestic military mobilization.

23
00:04:00.785 --> 00:04:12.067
The day after Kim returned, a Chinese veterans affairs ministry delegation led by a vice minister-level official met with North Korea's Urban Management Minister Kim Son Il in Pyongyang.

24
00:04:12.067 --> 00:04:19.710
The commemorative framing and the follow-on delegation suggest this was a structured diplomatic exchange, not just a day trip.

25
00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:33.922
Third, KUIC earns its first credit ratings. The Korea-U.S. Strategic Investment Corporation, known as KUIC, has received its first sovereign-linked credit ratings since launching earlier this year.

26
00:04:33.922 --> 00:04:43.697
Moody's assigned an Aa2 rating with a stable outlook. S and P Global Ratings assigned AA. Both are in line with South Korea's own sovereign ratings.

27
00:04:43.697 --> 00:04:56.180
Moody's cited the South Korean government's, quote, "very high willingness and ability to provide timely support, given the strong credit linkages between KUIC and the Government of Korea."

28
00:04:56.180 --> 00:05:08.465
KUIC is the state-run vehicle created by the National Assembly's Special Investment Act of March 12, 2026, to implement Seoul's 350-billion-dollar investment pledge to the United States.

29
00:05:08.465 --> 00:05:13.947
The ratings matter because they establish the borrowing credibility of the vehicle.

30
00:05:13.947 --> 00:05:28.280
Sovereign-equivalent ratings mean KUIC can access international capital markets on terms close to what Seoul itself would get, which directly affects how efficiently that 350-billion-dollar commitment can be deployed.

31
00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:33.960
Watch for how KUIC uses these ratings to structure its first major financing activity.

32
00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:42.209
Now to Pyongyang. State media today carried no new Kim Jong Un appearances. He was last seen publicly one day ago.

33
00:05:42.209 --> 00:05:53.154
The top coverage from Pyongyang led with aftermath reporting on Kim's Dandong visit, invoking what state media called the "blood alliance" framing between North Korea and China.

34
00:05:53.154 --> 00:06:04.779
State media also amplified North Korea's letter to the U.N. Security Council claiming the demilitarized zone mine blast was staged by Seoul. Those two threads, the China visit and the U.N.

35
00:06:04.779 --> 00:06:08.860
accusation, are the frame Pyongyang is putting around this moment.

36
00:06:10.060 --> 00:06:22.710
The rest of the news, quickly. A U.S. District Court for the District of Columbia has ordered North Korea to pay 400 million dollars to compensate USS Pueblo crew members over the 1968 seizure of the ship.

37
00:06:22.710 --> 00:06:32.583
That brings total damages related to the incident to nearly 3 billion dollars. The Pueblo has been docked in Pyongyang as a museum exhibit since the late 1990s.

38
00:06:32.583 --> 00:06:47.207
On the economy, South Korean small and medium enterprise exports hit an all-time quarterly high of 33.4 billion dollars in the third quarter, with cumulative January-through-September exports reaching 96.5 billion dollars, also a record.

39
00:06:47.207 --> 00:06:56.463
K-beauty drove much of the expansion, pushing into Latin American and other emerging markets. President Lee Jae-myung highlighted those figures today.

40
00:06:56.463 --> 00:07:13.926
President Lee also told a meeting with farmers and fishermen that joining the Comprehensive and Progressive Agreement for Trans-Pacific Partnership was "unavoidable" for South Korea, but pledged the country would not apply this year and would consult agricultural stakeholders first.

41
00:07:13.926 --> 00:07:20.467
Lee said South Korea would "not unilaterally impose sacrifice on people with weak power and small voices."

42
00:07:20.467 --> 00:07:36.202
At the National Assembly's judiciary committee audit, ruling Democratic Party and opposition People Power Party lawmakers clashed over Chief Justice Cho Hui-dae's refusal to re-nominate a justice and over the appellate court ruling in the Oh Se-hoon case.

43
00:07:36.202 --> 00:07:55.085
The Serious Crimes Investigation Agency director Kim Ji-yong told the Public Administration and Security Committee he would "need to check" whether to attend future cabinet meetings, a comment drawing criticism from People Power Party lawmakers who called it a signal of eroding investigative independence.

44
00:07:55.085 --> 00:08:02.613
The Bank of Korea appointed Choi Chang-ho, director of the monetary policy bureau, as deputy governor for monetary policy.

45
00:08:02.613 --> 00:08:12.794
The Bank of Korea also signaled that household lending thresholds will remain tight in the fourth quarter, even as demand for housing loans is projected to increase.

46
00:08:12.794 --> 00:08:18.348
Seoul apartment prices rose 0.61 percent in October, the slowest monthly gain of the year.

47
00:08:18.348 --> 00:08:27.110
SK Hynix is weighing a special dividend of 12.1 billion dollars, according to the Korea Herald, following strong AI-driven memory chip demand.

48
00:08:27.110 --> 00:08:36.860
SK Hynix third-quarter earnings are due Wednesday, and Samsung Electronics reports Thursday. The Federal Reserve's two-day policy meeting also begins Tuesday.

49
00:08:37.460 --> 00:08:50.904
In the region, Australia, Japan, New Zealand, and South Korea are questioning the value of their Indo-Pacific Four format within NATO structures, according to Intelligence Online, citing shifting U.S.

50
00:08:50.904 --> 00:09:00.248
security priorities. Gallup Korea, in a poll taken October 13 to 15, put President Lee's approval at 39 percent, up from the prior reading.

51
00:09:00.248 --> 00:09:07.710
The Democratic Party stood at 36 percent, the People Power Party at 27 percent, and independents at 29 percent.

52
00:09:08.910 --> 00:09:21.112
Beyond the region. India's Union Minister Sarbananda Sonowal visited Busan to push shipbuilding cooperation with South Korea, with India targeting a top-five global shipbuilding ranking by 2047.

53
00:09:21.112 --> 00:09:34.006
The International Atomic Energy Agency Director General Rafael Grossi canceled his planned visit to South Korea one day before a scheduled nuclear energy ministerial meeting, with no reason given publicly.

54
00:09:34.006 --> 00:09:44.510
And South Korea is competing against the United States and France for a Czech nuclear reactor contract, one of Seoul's highest-profile overseas nuclear export targets.

55
00:09:45.710 --> 00:09:59.114
Finally, the markets. Seoul's main stock index, the KOSPI, closed down about one and a half percent to around 5,469. The won traded at about 1,324 to the dollar, nearly flat.

56
00:09:59.114 --> 00:10:13.520
Brent crude fell sharply, down about 6 percent to around 110 dollars a barrel. The Bank of Korea's policy rate stands at 2.50 percent. That's the Korea Daily Brief for Monday, October 26.

57
00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:18.450
The full text, with a link to every source, is in today's email.
